CloudBeatz — Deployment Guide

This short guide contains the exact commands and steps you used to build & deploy your Vite app to Firebase Hosting. Save it and reuse next time.

---

1. Prerequisites

* Node.js & npm installed
* Firebase CLI installed and logged in:
  npm install -g firebase-tools
  firebase login
* Your project initialized with firebase CLI (firebase init hosting) or already has firebase.json & .firebaserc

2. Environment (Vite)

* Put secrets in `.env` with `VITE_` prefix (local dev and build-time replacement):
  VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=...
  VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=...

3. Typical package.json scripts (if missing)
   {
   "scripts": {
   "dev": "vite",
   "build": "vite build",
   "preview": "vite preview"
   }
   }

4. Build (create production-ready files)

# from project root (where package.json is)

npm install   # if needed
npm run build

# After this you'll have a `dist/` folder

5. firebase.json (set hosting public to dist)
   {
   "hosting": {
   "public": "dist",
   "ignore": ["firebase.json","**/.*","**/node_modules/**"],
   "rewrites": [
   { "source": "**", "destination": "/index.html" }
   ]
   }
   }

6. Deploy to Firebase Hosting

# make sure firebase project is selected (or use --project <id>)

firebase use --add   # optional, to set active project
firebase deploy --only hosting

7. Verify

* Open hosting URL (example: [https://PROJECT.web.app](https://PROJECT.web.app))
* Hard reload: Ctrl+Shift+R
* DevTools → Network → verify .js and .css files are served from /assets and return 200 (not HTML)

8. Common issues & fixes

* Unexpected token '<' when loading JS
  -> Means the requested JS returned HTML (usually index.html). Fix: ensure `dist/assets/...` exists and firebase `public` points to `dist`.
* import.meta.env is undefined
  -> You deployed raw index.html that contains `import.meta.env` placeholders. Build with Vite so env vars are replaced at build time.
* 404 on assets
  -> Check paths in index.html (absolute vs relative) and confirm assets are present in dist folder.

9. Optional: Auto-deploy using GitHub Actions (basic)
   Create `.github/workflows/firebase-hosting.yml` with:

name: Deploy to Firebase
on:
push:
branches: [ main ]

jobs:
build-and-deploy:
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
steps:
- uses: actions/checkout@v4
- name: Use Node.js
uses: actions/setup-node@v4
with:
node-version: 18
- name: Install deps
run: npm ci
- name: Build
run: npm run build
- name: Deploy to Firebase
uses: FirebaseExtended/action-hosting-deploy@v0
with:
repoToken: "${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}"
firebaseServiceAccount: "${{ secrets.FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT }}"
channelId: live

Notes: create a Firebase service account JSON and add it to GitHub Secrets as FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT (base64 or raw JSON as action expects).

10. Quick checklist before deploy

* npm run build => dist created
* firebase.json public set to "dist"
* index.html scripts reference built assets (usually handled by Vite)
* env vars present in .env (VITE_ prefix) before build
* firebase deploy --only hosting

